{"war":"soviet-afghan-war","start":"1979-12-24","end":"1989-07-31","note":"There were no front lines: the government and Soviet forces held the cities and main roads, the mujahideen much of the countryside, and control shifted constantly. Areas show only a few well-documented cases (the Panjshir valley, besieged government towns after 1987) and are approximate.","chapters":[{"id":"invasion","title":"The Soviet invasion, December 1979","start":"1979-12-24","end":"1980-01-31","bbox":[60.5,29.3,75.0,38.6],"arrows":["Soviet 40th Army: Termez to Kabul"],"text":"Afghanistan's communist government faced a growing revolt, and in September 1979 Hafizullah Amin seized power. On 25 December the Soviet 40th Army began crossing the border, flying troops into Kabul and Bagram and driving south along the road from Termez to Kabul. On 27 December Soviet special forces stormed the Tajbeg Palace, killed Amin and installed Babrak Karmal. The government (blue) held the cities, but much of the countryside was already slipping out of its control, which the map cannot show."},{"id":"panjshir","title":"The Panjshir offensives","start":"1980-04-09","end":"1984-09-30","bbox":[68.9,34.95,70.5,36.0],"arrows":["Panjshir V offensive","Panjshir VII offensive"],"text":"The Panjshir valley, 70 km north of Kabul and close to the Salang road, was the stronghold of Ahmad Shah Massoud's mujahideen. Soviet and Afghan forces attacked it nine times; in May 1982 (Panjshir V) they set up bases at Rukha, Bazarak and Anava, but held only the valley floor. A truce in January 1983 left only a small garrison at Anava, and after the huge Panjshir VII offensive of April 1984 Soviet forces again withdrew, keeping only the lower valley. Each time the mujahideen came back."},{"id":"zhawar-stinger","title":"Zhawar and the first Stingers","start":"1986-02-28","end":"1986-09-30","bbox":[69.0,32.85,71.2,34.7],"arrows":["Afghan and Soviet offensive on Zhawar"],"text":"Near the Pakistani border, Jalaluddin Haqqani's mujahideen ran a supply and training base in caves at Zhawar, while the government garrison of Khost had been cut off since 1981. Afghan troops with Soviet support took Zhawar on 19 April 1986 after seven weeks, but withdrew within hours and the mujahideen soon rebuilt it. On 25 September 1986, near Jalalabad, mujahideen shot down the first Soviet helicopter with a US-supplied Stinger missile. Most of this region was contested, so control is not mapped."},{"id":"magistral","title":"Operation Magistral: the road to Khost","start":"1987-11-19","end":"1988-02-05","bbox":[68.9,32.95,70.4,34.0],"arrows":["Operation Magistral"],"text":"From 1987 the map shows most of the country as mujahideen ground, so the government's towns appear as separate areas. To relieve besieged Khost, Soviet and Afghan forces attacked from Gardez on 19 November 1987, stormed the Sata-Kandow pass in early December and entered Khost on 30 December. Their outposts along the road were withdrawn at the end of January 1988, and the mujahideen cut Khost off again."},{"id":"withdrawal","title":"The Geneva Accords and the Soviet withdrawal","start":"1988-04-14","end":"1989-02-15","bbox":[60.5,29.3,75.0,38.6],"arrows":["Soviet withdrawal over the Friendship Bridge"],"text":"Under the Geneva Accords of 14 April 1988, the Soviet Union agreed to leave. Withdrawal began on 15 May 1988, and on 15 February 1989 General Boris Gromov, commander of the 40th Army, was the last to walk across the Friendship Bridge to the Soviet Union. About 14,500 Soviet soldiers had died in the war, and up to 2 million Afghans. President Najibullah's government still held Kabul and the main cities."},{"id":"jalalabad-1989","title":"Jalalabad, 1989","start":"1989-03-05","end":"1989-07-15","bbox":[69.6,33.9,71.3,34.75],"arrows":["Mujahideen assault on Jalalabad","Afghan Army retakes Samarkhel"],"text":"Expecting the government to collapse once the Soviets had gone, mujahideen groups backed by Pakistan's intelligence service attacked Jalalabad on 5 March 1989. They took the airfield and the army base at Samarkhel, but were stopped at the city's defences, while the Afghan air force and Scud missiles struck them. By July the army had retaken Samarkhel; the failure showed that the government could survive without Soviet troops, and it lasted until 1992."}],"fronts":[],"occupations":[{"name":"Massoud's Panjshir valley (mujahideen-held)","side":1,"end":"1986-12-31","keys":[{"date":"1980-04-08","coords":[[69.78,35.11],[69.96,35.23],[69.96,35.3],[69.94,35.33],[69.95,35.37],[69.99,35.38],[70.06,35.45],[70.11,35.46],[70.15,35.5],[70.15,35.54],[70.18,35.56],[70.19,35.61],[70.25,35.65],[70.24,35.83],[70.23,35.88],[70.2,35.91],[70.12,35.92],[70.05,35.86],[69.93,35.83],[69.9,35.79],[69.76,35.73],[69.69,35.65],[69.69,35.55],[69.48,35.52],[69.4,35.47],[69.25,35.46],[69.17,35.34],[69.17,35.3],[69.2,35.27],[69.58,35.27],[69.61,35.25],[69.73,35.25],[69.75,35.23],[69.75,35.17]]},{"date":"1982-05-15","coords":[[69.78,35.11],[69.96,35.23],[69.96,35.3],[69.94,35.33],[69.95,35.37],[69.99,35.38],[70.06,35.45],[70.11,35.46],[70.15,35.5],[70.15,35.54],[70.18,35.56],[70.19,35.61],[70.25,35.65],[70.24,35.83],[70.23,35.88],[70.2,35.91],[70.12,35.92],[70.05,35.86],[69.93,35.83],[69.9,35.79],[69.76,35.73],[69.69,35.65],[69.69,35.55],[69.48,35.52],[69.4,35.47],[69.25,35.46],[69.17,35.34],[69.17,35.3],[69.2,35.27],[69.58,35.27],[69.61,35.25],[69.73,35.25],[69.75,35.23],[69.75,35.17]]},{"date":"1982-06-10","coords":[[69.78,35.11],[69.96,35.23],[69.96,35.3],[69.94,35.33],[69.95,35.37],[69.99,35.38],[70.06,35.45],[70.11,35.46],[70.15,35.5],[70.15,35.54],[70.18,35.56],[70.19,35.61],[70.25,35.65],[70.24,35.83],[70.23,35.88],[70.2,35.91],[70.12,35.92],[70.05,35.86],[69.93,35.83],[69.9,35.79],[69.76,35.73],[69.69,35.65],[69.69,35.55],[69.48,35.52],[69.4,35.47],[69.25,35.46],[69.17,35.34],[69.17,35.3],[69.2,35.27],[69.37,35.27],[69.48,35.35],[69.65,35.4],[69.71,35.4],[69.73,35.37],[69.71,35.32],[69.56,35.29],[69.54,35.27],[69.61,35.25],[69.7